IA: violations by stop position in route = {8: 4, 9: 29, 10: 180, 11: 116, 12: 11, 13: 59, 14: 488}
Every violation in IA has a negative delta: occupancy falls below previous + boarded - alighted,
i.e. passengers leave the bus without being counted as alightings. They are spread over the day
(not only end of service). They are kept in all analyses (plan: violations are not removed).

A3 - Waiting-time consistency
-----------------------------
                                            check         IA        AG
                                    waiting times    9396837   9409638
                                   negative waits          0         0
                                    missing waits          0         0
                    visits with #waits == boarded  100.000 % 100.000 %
                                     wait max (s)     8354.0    9480.0
                                       wait > 1 h       2576      3205
                      median headway at stops (s)      776.0     772.0
waits longer than the time since the previous bus     7.85 %    5.25 %
   ... of which the previous bus denied boardings    32.99 %   61.48 %
         ... previous bus had room (inconsistent)     489369    188502
                     inconsistent: % of all waits     5.21 %    2.00 %
    inconsistent: excess over headway p50/p90 (s) 266 / 1118   28 / 90
inconsistent: at bunched visits (headway < 120 s)     59.5 %    24.4 %
One waiting time is recorded per boarded passenger; denied passengers have no waiting time at that visit.
A wait longer than the time since the previous bus at the same stop means the passenger was already
queueing when that bus passed. This should only happen if that bus was full (denied boardings > 0):
the check verifies that left-behind passengers stay in the queue and keep their accumulated wait.
Finding: some passengers wait longer than the time since a previous bus that still had room.
  AG: the excess is of the order of a dwell time (median ~30 s): stop times are recorded at departure,
      so passengers arriving while the previous bus was dwelling appear to have waited longer than the
      headway. Timing convention, not an error.
  IA: the excess is much larger (minutes) and concentrates on bunched buses. In the IA model a bus asks
      the stop to notify waiting passengers on arrival and only boards the passengers that answer that
      broadcast with a 'Board me' request within 3 cycles (handle_stop_response_and_boards). Passengers
      arriving after the broadcast, or not served when two buses are at the stop, stay in the queue even
      if the bus has room. This is a behavioural difference of the IA protocol that lengthens IA waits.
No passenger arrival timestamps are written, so waits cannot be re-derived independently.

